{"name":"Fish Story","hex":"#258f90","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fish story","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An exaggerated or boastful story."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fish_story"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fish Story (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Fish Story  is a 2009 Japanese action comedy film directed by Yoshihiro Nakamura with the screenplay adapted from the homonymous novel by Kōtarō Isaka."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/3/39/Fish_Story_%28film%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fish_Story_(film)"},{"title":"Tall tale","blocks":[{"text":"A tall tale is a story with unbelievable elements, told as if it were true and factual. Some tall tales are exaggerations of actual events, for example fish stories such as, \"That fish was so big it nearly sank the boat when I pulled it in!\" Other tall tales are completely fictional tales set in a familiar setting, such as the European countryside, the American frontier, the Canadian Northwest, the Australian outback, or the beginning of the Industrial Revolution."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tall_tale"},{"title":"Fish","blocks":[{"text":"A fish is an aquatic, anamniotic, gill-bearing vertebrate animal with a tough cranium to protect the brain, but lacking limbs with digits. Fish can be grouped into the more basal jawless fish and the more common jawed fish, the latter including all living cartilaginous and bony fish, as well as the extinct placoderms and acanthodians. Tetrapods are descended from fish, so in a break from the long tradition of grouping all fish into a single class (Pisces), modern phylogenetics views fish as a paraphyletic group that includes all vertebrates except tetrapods. To treat fish as a monophyletic group, one would have to classify tetrapods as a clade/subgroup of specialized fish, which would make \"fish\" and \"vertebrates\" synonyms. In English, the plural of \"fish\" is fish when referring to individuals and fishes when referring to species."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Balantiocheilos_melanopterus_-_Karlsruhe_Zoo_02_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Balantiocheilos_melanopterus_-_Karlsruhe_Zoo_02_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fish"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Klamath-CA-Babe.jpg/500px-Klamath-CA-Babe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Klamath-CA-Babe.jpg/1280px-Klamath-CA-Babe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Klamath-CA-Babe.jpg/500px-Klamath-CA-Babe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Paul Bunyan's sidekick, Babe the blue ox, sculpted as a ten-meter tall roadside tourist-attraction","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKlamath-CA-Babe.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Giant%27s_Causeway_%2813%29.JPG/500px-Giant%27s_Causeway_%2813%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Giant%27s_Causeway_%2813%29.JPG/1280px-Giant%27s_Causeway_%2813%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Giant%27s_Causeway_%2813%29.JPG/500px-Giant%27s_Causeway_%2813%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Columnar basalt that makes up the Giant's Causeway; in legend, a fine set of hexagonal stepping stones to Scotland, made by Fionn mac Cumhaill","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A